{
  "title": { "en": "Cantor" },
  "tray": "proof",
  "after": {
    "en": "This is <b>Cantor’s theorem</b>: a set always has more subsets than elements."
  },
  "tasks": [
    {
      "id": "u-cantor",
      "kind": "proof",
      "tests": [
        { "premises": { "s": "∀z. ∃x. ∀y. P(y, f(x)) ↔ P(y, z)", "d": "∃z. ∀y. P(y, z) ↔ ¬P(y, f(y))" }, "goal": "⊥" }
      ],
      "cases": [{ "P": "In" }],
      "brief": {
        "en": "f gives each x a set f(x). Every set is some f(x) (f is onto); and there is a set z holding exactly the y not in f(y). Get the contradiction ⊥."
      },
      "hint": [
        { "en": "Take out z first, then the x whose f(x) is z." },
        { "en": "Taking y = x in both gives X ↔ Y and Y ↔ ¬X." },
        {
          "en": "From these two, build what <part-ref to=\"lemma:liar\"></part-ref> needs."
        }
      ],
      "solution": [
        "e1 = exE[x=a](d, body: e2) {",
        "  body {",
        "    a1 = allE[t=a](s)",
        "    e2 = exE[x=b](a1, body: l) {",
        "      body {",
        "        a3 = allE[t=b](e1.body)",
        "        a2 = allE[t=b](e2.body)",
        "        s2 = andE(a3)",
        "        s1 = andE(a2)",
        "        c1 = chain(s1.left, s2.left)",
        "        c2 = chain(s2.right, s1.right)",
        "        j = andI(c1, c2)",
        "        l = liar(j)",
        "      }",
        "    }",
        "  }",
        "}",
        "goal <- e1"
      ],
      "naive": [
        "e1 = exE[x=a](d, body: l) {",
        "  body {",
        "    a3 = allE[t=a](e1.body)",
        "    l = liar(a3)",
        "  }",
        "}",
        "goal <- e1"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
